## Restore From History

The existing endpoint from Task 05 already restores a historical version.

Reuse it.

Conceptually:

User views Area at currentVersion = 6
    ↓
selects Restore on v2
    ↓
POST .../versions/2/restore
{
  "expectedVersion": 6
}
    ↓
backend creates v7 RESTORED
    ↓
Area becomes snapshot from v2
    ↓
history refreshes
    ↓
v7 appears as current

Do NOT:

- change currentVersion back to 2;
- mutate v2;
- delete versions 3–6;
- create a second restore implementation.

History remains append-only.

---

## Restore OCC

The History UI must use the current version known when the restore action
is initiated.

If another mutation happens before restore:

history loaded with currentVersion = 6

another client updates Area:

currentVersion = 7

first client tries:

restore v2 expectedVersion=6

Expected:

409 VERSION_CONFLICT

Do not silently retry restore using version 7.

Do not automatically overwrite the newer state.

For the prototype, reuse the existing conflict-handling principles:

- inform the user;
- reload authoritative Area/history state.

Propose the exact frontend behavior.

